          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Usually no. In the ICD-10-CM passages I retrieved, the three-character category is the start of a code, not the full code. The one case the sources describe as complete at three characters is I10 (essential hypertension).\n\n**What the sources say:**\n\n- **The three-character form is the category.** Characters four to seven add detail such as cause, site, severity, laterality, and encounter type. A decimal point follows the third character when more characters follow. (ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions)\n- **Most categories need more characters.** The hypertension summary says most ICD-10-CM categories require a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code is billable. I10 takes none, so it is the exception. (Coding hypertension: I10)\n- **Diabetes and COPD follow the same pattern.** E11 and J44 are categories with subdivisions. For J44, the fourth character records whether a complication is present. (Coding type 2 diabetes; Coding COPD)\n\n**What the sources do not cover:** The passages I retrieved do not state the general rule in one sentence, such as \"category headers are non-billable.\" They also do not list which specific categories are complete at three characters, apart from I10. So I can't confirm the full rule from these sources alone.\n\n**Other points from the sources:**\n\n- Code sets differ by country and by year. A code valid in one version may be retired or changed in another, so check any code against a current official file before relying on it. (ICD-10-CM versus WHO ICD-10)\n- If you are submitting codes for a real claim or record, the people who should settle the question are a qualified medical coder or your payer's coding guidance. They can apply the current official rules to your case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
